  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "The variant gives wrapped bytes an in-band payload type via pCID and makes failures explicit, but it does not make CID codec identity alone distinguish raw chunks from pointer objects; that question is deferred to the envelope context or the payload protocol.",
    "strengths": [
      "Payload interpretation is bound in-band by pCID rather than local filenames or paths.",
      "Deterministic CBOR plus mandatory signatures support reproducible auditing.",
      "Envelope, payload, and signature responsibilities are separated clearly."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "CID codec identity alone still does not tell Bob whether bytes are a pointer object or raw payload.",
      "Type binding for separately addressed body bytes is pushed into the payload protocol, not solved at the envelope layer.",
      "Unknown-hard-reject makes the design brittle across future protocol additions."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Older peers may drop or refuse future pCIDs instead of preserving evidence for later handling.",
      "If the envelope is stripped or only payload bytes are shared by hash, the type signal can disappear.",
      "The draft is unfrozen and the spec pCID is not yet minted, so long-term interoperability remains uncertain."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "What outer codec or object convention tells Bob that arbitrary bytes should be parsed as this grid envelope at all?",
      "How are raw-chunk versus pointer-object distinctions carried when only a referenced CAS blob survives, not the whole envelope?",
      "Can unknown-hard-reject coexist with archival or relay behavior needed for long-lived mixed-version networks?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
}
